s its upkeep plus 6%, its store sat at the line it dies at, and a body takes on only some of its decisions, so a meal must last several intervals); with satiety past `full` only, seeds 1 and 2 still lost all their eaters and seed 3 kept them; at 3 × 10^4 seeds 1 and 2 kept them. The founders had neutral brains and starve within weeks, so a small group lived only if it bred faster than it starved until it learned: 97 to 99% of the placed eaters died in their first year, and everything after descended from 2 to 14 founders. Review 6 (finding 1) showed that the number was set to win that lottery, and that it made every life 3 times shorter (a 57 kg eater needs 82 days for a child at 3 × 10^4, 245 at 10^4; a measured life was 0.25 years). **The start is hand-set instead, because neutral founders are a lottery, and no law is set for a start any more:** `ew run` and `ew run --roster` place every animal kind with the hand-set eater biases (`Seeding::hand[1]`; `--neutral-eaters` for the neutral start, `--plant-bias` for hand-set plants, which start neutral; the config line of every run says which start it had). With them and 10^4 the fewest founder bodies were 25 to 77 (review 6, 64x64, seeds 1 to 3) against 2 to 14 at 3 × 10^4. The number is anchored (1,500 to 30,000) and marked tuned in `docs/numbers.md` as a choice within the anchor that runs once decided. A 57 kg eater (20.8 kg^0.75) then needs about 245 days for a 17 kg child (51 MJ of work). A plant's child costs only what is not liquid (14 Oct), so a 2.4 kg plant member owes about 8 × 10^5 J per child, about two weeks of its build: the law barely slows plants. The CHILD gene lets a kind choose small frequent children or large rare ones. This is the cause that stands for a maturity range (review 5, section 3) |
